## Why This Is Asked

Tests critical authorization paths, data-loss prevention, and audit integrity in multi-tenant setups, plus resilience to flaky services and retention policies.

## Key Concepts

- RBAC and DLP enforcement across tenants
- Tamper-evident audit logs (sequencing, signatures/HMAC)
- End-to-end data export redaction
- Seeds and mocks for deterministic, isolated tests
- Flaky service handling and time-based retention checks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo end-to-end test sketch (Playwright/Cypress-agnostic)
async function testRBACAndDLP() {
  const t = seedTenant('tenantA');
  const uOwner = seedUser(t, 'owner');
  const uEditor = seedUser(t, 'editor');
  const uAuditor = seedUser(t, 'auditor');
  const admin = seedUser(t, 'admin');

  // Owner creates a share with PII
  await login(uOwner);
  await createShare({ tenant: t.id, data: 'PII' });

  // Editor attempts restricted action
  await login(uEditor);
  assertAccessDenied(editShare(t.id));

  // Auditor reads audit trail
  await login(uAuditor);
  assertAuditIncludes(t.id, 'READ', uAuditor.id);

  // Admin exports with redaction
  await login(admin);
  const exportPayload = await exportData(t.id);
  assertRedacts(exportPayload, ['PII']);
  // Verify tamper-evident log
  assertTamperEvidence(t.id);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Feature-flag testing in multi-tenant, privacy-focused applications is common at scale. This question evaluates understanding of determinism, data isolation, and resilience under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant test isolation
- Feature-flag gating and consent API mocking
- Tenant-scoped headers and data boundaries
- Resilience: 503 handling with exponential backoff
- Desktop and mobile parity in UI and data latency

## Code Example

```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('privacy flag rollout across tenants', async ({ page }) => {
  // Seed tenants A (flag on) and B (flag off)
  const tenantA = await seedTenant({ dataSharingEnabled: true });
  const tenantB = await seedTenant({ dataSharingEnabled: false });

  // Mock feature-flag and consent services
  await page.route('**/api/feature-flags', (route) => {
    const tenantId = route.request().headers()['x-tenant-id'];
    route.fulfill({
      status: 200,
      contentType: 'application/json',
      body: JSON.stringify({
        dataSharing: tenantId === tenantA.id
      })
    });
  });

  await page.route('**/api/consent', (route) => {
    route.fulfill({ status: 200, contentType: 'application/json', body: JSON.stringify({ granted: true }) });
  });

  // Verify tenant A sees dashboard
  await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantA.id } });
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-content"]')).toBeVisible();

  // Verify tenant B is blocked
  await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantB.id } });
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-content"]')).not.toBeVisible();

  // Validate tenant-scoped headers in API calls
  const apiRequest = page.waitForRequest('**/api/analytics/**');
  await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantA.id } });
  expect((await apiRequest).headers()['x-tenant-id']).toBe(tenantA.id);

  // Test 503 retry with exponential backoff
  let attempts = 0;
  await page.route('**/api/analytics/**', (route) => {
    attempts++;
    if (attempts < 3) {
      route.fulfill({ status: 503 });
    } else {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ latency: 120 }) });
    }
  });

  // Desktop and mobile parity
  for (const viewport of [{ width: 1280, height: 720 }, { width: 375, height: 667 }]) {
    await page.setViewportSize(viewport);
    await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantA.id } });
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-content"]')).toBeVisible();
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-latency"]')).toHaveText(/\d+ms/);
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for real-time collaboration across multiple clients require reasoning about concurrency, conflict resolution, and fault handling in production-like conditions.

## Key Concepts

- WebSocket end-to-end flows across clients
- CRDT vs OT conflict resolution guarantees
- Autosave cadence and durability in a shared store
- Test data isolation and per-document teardown
- Latency and network fault injection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test harness for 3 clients editing same doc
async function testConcurrentEdits(docId){
  const [c1,c2,c3] = await Promise.all([openClient(docId), openClient(docId), openClient(docId)]);
  await c1.edit('A');
  await c2.edit('B');
  await c3.edit('C');
  await wait(500);
  const finalContent = await getDocContent(docId);
  // expect CRDT-merge result reflects A,B,C edits
}
```

